# magnetite-runtime/deploy/Caddyfile.example
#
# Terminates TLS in front of a magnetite node so a browser on an `https://`
# page can open `wss://` to it. The node itself (magnetite-runtime/src/server.rs)
# speaks plain `ws://` only and has no TLS of its own — this proxy is what
# supplies the `wss://` layer, not a change to the node.
#
# Same shape as flowstock's and pango's own public-node recipes
# (flowstock/docs/CLOUD-NODE.md §3, pango/docs/CLOUD-NODE.md §5): Caddy on the
# same host, the node bound to loopback so the proxy is the only path in.
#
# Usage:
#   1. Start the node on loopback only:
#        magnetite node --wasm game.wasm --host 127.0.0.1 --port 9000
#      (or, for a smoke test with no game module:
#        magnetite-serve --host 127.0.0.1 --port 9000)
#   2. Point DNS for your-node.example.com at this host, then:
#        caddy run --config Caddyfile.example
#      Caddy provisions and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for the real
#      domain automatically; open only 80 (ACME) and 443.
#   3. Clients connect to wss://your-node.example.com — never to port 9000
#      directly, which should not be reachable from outside this host.
#
# For a LOCAL verification with no domain and no internet-routable host,
# replace the site address with `localhost` and the directive `tls internal`
# — Caddy mints its own local CA and a certificate for `localhost`, which is
# enough to prove the wss:// code path end-to-end without owning a domain.
# This is exactly how the recipe below was verified; see
# docs/hosting-a-server.md "Running players over wss://" for the pasted
# handshake output.
#
#   localhost:9443 {
#       tls internal
#       reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000
#   }

your-node.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000
}
